WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.105
Filenames are often overloaded.

2
00:00:02.185 --> 00:00:05.855
The same value may be used for display,
storage, routing, download headers,

3
00:00:05.935 --> 00:00:07.712
logging, and processing decisions.

4
00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:11.576
Those are different purposes and should
not collapse into one trusted string.

5
00:00:11.656 --> 00:00:15.383
A user-supplied filename is untrusted
display data, not a safe storage path.

6
00:00:15.463 --> 00:00:18.674
Generate server-side filenames, object
keys, or IDs for storage,

7
00:00:18.754 --> 00:00:22.882
and preserve the original filename only as
metadata when the application truly needs

8
00:00:22.962 --> 00:00:23.134
it.

9
00:00:23.214 --> 00:00:26.367
Original filenames can contain long
values, confusing Unicode,

10
00:00:26.447 --> 00:00:29.429
path separators, reserved names,
hidden-file conventions,

11
00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:33.522
double extensions, control-like
characters, and platform-specific surprises.

12
00:00:33.602 --> 00:00:36.526
Even when a framework exposes a convenient
filename method,

13
00:00:36.606 --> 00:00:39.416
the value came from the client or another
external source.

14
00:00:39.496 --> 00:00:43.165
Apply length limits and restrict displayed
characters where appropriate.

15
00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:46.628
Escape or encode filenames before placing
them into HTML, JSON, logs,

16
00:00:46.708 --> 00:00:49.575
response headers, templates, dashboards,
or audit views.

17
00:00:49.655 --> 00:00:52.005
File type decisions also need layered
thinking.

18
00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:55.239
Do not trust a file extension or declared
Content-Type by itself.

19
00:00:55.319 --> 00:00:58.415
Use allowlists for the types required by
the business function,

20
00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:02.508
and validate with multiple signals where
appropriate: extension, declared type,

21
00:01:02.588 --> 00:01:05.455
file signature, parser behavior, and
expected structure.

22
00:01:05.535 --> 00:01:07.771
The depth of validation should match the
risk.

23
00:01:07.851 --> 00:01:10.718
A public static asset workflow, a private
report download,

24
00:01:10.798 --> 00:01:13.263
and an archive import job all have
different needs.

25
00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:14.662
Metadata can be sensitive.

26
00:01:14.742 --> 00:01:18.010
Original filename, uploader, tenant ID,
storage key, scan status,

27
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.269
processing result, content type, file
size,

28
00:01:20.349 --> 00:01:22.986
and retention state can reveal business
information.

29
00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:26.449
Treat metadata as part of the file record,
not as harmless decoration.

30
00:01:26.529 --> 00:01:30.256
A safe design separates storage identity,
display metadata, access policy,

31
00:01:30.336 --> 00:01:34.407
and processing decisions so a mistake in
one area does not automatically compromise

32
00:01:34.487 --> 00:01:35.060
the others.
